\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces The \textsf  {R} entry. (a)--(c) Rules R1--R3 at the top-left corner of the grid, which continues to the right and below. Green is a colour $\gamma $ and orange the other colour $\bar  \gamma $. The forced path pieces are drawn, settled cells are grey, and dashed circles are the effective endpoints. (d), (e) Two $11 \times 12$ instances on which \textsf  {R} fires and no other entry does. Neither has a solution. After the forced routes, the four effective endpoints lie on the border of the remaining region (shaded purple) and alternate in colour along it. This is the \textsf  {T1} condition for that region, so the two paths would have to cross. In (d) rule R1 applies at three corners, and in (e) rule R3 settles eight cells at the top-right corner and R1 settles two at the top-left.\relax }}{8}{figure.caption.15}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:R}{{4}{8}{The \ent {R} entry. (a)--(c) Rules R1--R3 at the top-left corner of the grid, which continues to the right and below. Green is a colour $\gamma $ and orange the other colour $\bar \gamma $. The forced path pieces are drawn, settled cells are grey, and dashed circles are the effective endpoints. (d), (e) Two $11 \times 12$ instances on which \ent {R} fires and no other entry does. Neither has a solution. After the forced routes, the four effective endpoints lie on the border of the remaining region (shaded purple) and alternate in colour along it. This is the \ent {T1} condition for that region, so the two paths would have to cross. In (d) rule R1 applies at three corners, and in (e) rule R3 settles eight cells at the top-right corner and R1 settles two at the top-left.\relax }{figure.caption.15}{}}
